<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Speicherstadt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Speicherstadt is a warehouse district in the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, built from 1883 after the city agreed to join the German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> while keeping a free port. Its red-brick Gothic Revival warehouses, standing on oak piles between canals, form the largest such complex in the world, and about 20,000 people were moved to clear the site. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kontorhaus-district">Kontorhaus District</a> it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2015.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/speicherstadt.jpg" alt="Speicherstadt" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Speicherstadt_abends.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
